id: "neet-ray-optics",
title: "Ray Optics",
subject: "Physics",
tag: "Physics · High weightage",
blurb: "Reflection and refraction, mirrors and lenses, total internal reflection, and optical instruments.",
intro: "Ray optics treats light as rays travelling in straight lines in a homogeneous medium, reflecting and refracting at interfaces. Two laws govern everything: <strong>reflection</strong> (angle of incidence = angle of reflection, all in one plane) and <strong>refraction — Snell's law</strong>, μ1 sin i = μ2 sin r, where μ = c/v is the refractive index. From these follow mirrors, lenses, prisms, total internal reflection, and every optical instrument NEET asks about. Master the Cartesian sign convention once — light travels left to right, distances measured from the pole/optical centre, rightward positive — and every formula becomes mechanical.",
